    Section(
      title: '+ Compare wheelchair options for this patient: ultralight manual vs. power-assist vs. full power wheelchair. What factors guide the selection?',
      content:
          '**Wheelchair Type Selection Analysis**:\n\n**Option 1: Ultralight Manual Wheelchair (Rigid Frame)**\n- **Pros**: Lightest option (typically 12-18 lbs); best energy efficiency for propulsion; folding or rigid frame options; fits in car independently; preserves upper extremity strength and cardiovascular fitness; familiar to patient\n- **Cons**: Does NOT address the fundamental problem — continued shoulder overuse; shoulder pain will likely progress without intervention\n- **Indication**: If shoulder pain resolves with treatment and wheelchair optimization\n\n**Option 2: Power-Assist Wheels (e.g., SmartDrive, E-Motion, Twion)**\n- **Pros**: Reduces propulsion effort by 30-50%; maintains manual wheelchair skills and body; fits on existing ultralight frame; allows manual propulsion when desired (exercise, sport) with power assist for long distances/inclines; preserves car transfer independence\n- **Cons**: Adds weight to wheelchair (5-15 lbs depending on system); battery management; cost (\$5,000-8,000); may not be sufficient if shoulder pain is severe\n- **Indication**: **Best option for this patient** — reduces shoulder demand while preserving his independence, community mobility, and wheelchair basketball participation\n\n**Option 3: Full Power Wheelchair**\n- **Pros**: Eliminates propulsion-related shoulder stress; tilt-in-space and recline for pressure relief; power seat elevation available\n- **Cons**: Heavy (250-350+ lbs); cannot be self-loaded into car; requires van modification or wheelchair-accessible vehicle (\$50,000+); changes his functional identity; may accelerate deconditioning; psychological impact\n- **Indication**: If shoulder pain progresses to the point where manual propulsion is not feasible even with power assist; or if bilateral rotator cuff tears develop\n\n**Recommended Approach for This Patient**:\n1. **New ultralight rigid-frame manual wheelchair** (properly fitted) with **power-assist system** (e.g., SmartDrive MX2+) for daily use\n2. Keep an ultralight manual chair for wheelchair basketball and short-distance use\n3. Optimize wheelchair configuration to reduce shoulder demand (see below)\n4. Concurrent shoulder rehabilitation program\n\n**Key Configuration Optimizations to Reduce Shoulder Stress**:\n- **Axle position**: Move rear axle forward (center of gravity closer to rear wheels) to improve push efficiency — each push covers more distance with less force\n- **Seat height**: Optimize so that with hands resting on push rims at top dead center, elbow flexion is 100-120 degrees\n- **Camber**: 3-5 degrees of camber for improved ergonomic hand position and stability\n- **Push rim**: Coated/ergonomic push rims (natural-fit or surge) for improved grip with less force\n- **Weight**: Minimize total chair weight (titanium frame, lightweight components)',
