    Section(
      title: '+ What seating system (cushion and back) would you prescribe to address pressure injury prevention and postural support?',
      content:
          '**Cushion Selection for Pressure Injury Prevention**:\n\nGiven the history of Stage 2 sacral pressure injury and current elevated pressure mapping values:\n\n1. **Air-based cushion** (e.g., ROHO Quadtro Select or ROHO High Profile):\n- Best pressure redistribution — air cells conform to body contours\n- Adjustable inflation for individualized pressure optimization\n- **ROHO Quadtro Select**: 4-zone adjustability allows targeted pressure reduction under ITs and sacrum\n- Peak pressures typically reduced to <40-60 mmHg\n- Requires ongoing maintenance (daily inflation check)\n\n2. **Hybrid cushion** (e.g., Jay Xtreme Active, Varilite):\n- Combines foam base with air/fluid overlay\n- Good pressure redistribution with more postural stability than pure air\n- Less maintenance than ROHO\n- Consider if patient finds pure air cushion unstable for transfers\n\n**Recommended**: ROHO Quadtro Select — best match for his pressure injury history and elevated IT pressures. Repeat pressure mapping with new cushion to verify adequate pressure redistribution.\n\n**Back Support System**:\n- Current sling back provides inadequate postural support → contributing to posterior pelvic tilt and kyphosis\n- **Prescription**: Rigid or semi-rigid back support (e.g., Jay J3 back or Ride Designs custom back)\n- Features needed: Adjustable lateral trunk supports to correct left lean; contoured posterior pelvic shelf to prevent posterior pelvic tilt; appropriate back height (below inferior scapular angle for C6 — preserves shoulder mobility for propulsion)\n\n**Additional Seating Components**:\n- **Anti-thrust seat**: Slight seat angle (dump) of 2-3 degrees to reduce posterior pelvic sliding\n- **Lap belt**: Two-point lap belt at 45-degree angle over pelvis (not abdomen) for pelvic positioning\n- **Armrests**: Desk-length, swing-away armrests for transfer clearance; consider removing for sports use\n\n**Pressure Relief Education Reinforcement**:\n- Weight shifts every 15-30 minutes (lean forward, lateral lean, or tilt)\n- With power-assist wheelchair: can potentially add tilt-in-space feature if future power chair conversion needed\n- Skin inspection twice daily with long-handled mirror\n- Cushion inflation check daily (for ROHO)',
