    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a shoulder preservation protocol for this SCI patient and the broader upper extremity preservation strategy.',
      content:
          '**Shoulder Preservation Protocol (Based on PVA/CSCM Clinical Practice Guidelines)**:\n\n**1. Rotator Cuff and Scapular Stabilization**:\n- **Strengthening**: External rotators (infraspinatus, teres minor) and scapular stabilizers (lower trapezius, serratus anterior, rhomboids). Use resistance bands or light free weights.\n- **Stretching**: Pectoral stretching (horizontal abduction), posterior capsule stretching (cross-body adduction). These address the typical SCI posture of protracted shoulders and tight anterior structures.\n- **Frequency**: 3 sets of 15 reps, 3-4 times/week. Low weight, high repetition to build endurance.\n- **Avoid**: Overhead exercises, heavy bench press, dips, and exercises that impinge the supraspinatus.\n\n**2. Propulsion Biomechanics Optimization**:\n- **Semicircular propulsion pattern**: Long, smooth strokes with hands dropping below the push rim on recovery (NOT the arc or pumping pattern which increases shoulder impingement)\n- **Reduce stroke frequency**: Fewer, more powerful strokes are less damaging than many short strokes\n- **Glove use**: Padded wheelchair gloves reduce grip force needed on push rims\n\n**3. Transfer Technique Modification**:\n- **Sliding board transfers**: Head-hips technique to reduce upper extremity loading\n- **Avoid**: Depression transfers (tricep-dip style) as they maximally load the shoulder in impingement position. Since this patient lacks triceps (C6), he already uses sliding board — reinforce technique.\n- **Transfer board**: Ensure smooth, low-friction board; consider Beasy board (curved, rotating) to reduce shoulder torque\n\n**4. Weight Management**:\n- Address the 8 kg weight gain — every kilogram of excess body weight increases shoulder loading during transfers and propulsion\n- Nutritional counseling for SCI-specific metabolism (reduced basal metabolic rate, increased visceral fat)\n- Accessible exercise: Arm ergometry, wheelchair basketball (modified intensity), swimming\n\n**5. Pain Management**:\n- Physical therapy for rotator cuff tendinopathy: eccentric exercises, manual therapy, modalities\n- NSAIDs for acute flares (short courses)\n- Subacromial corticosteroid injection if PT fails (limit to 3 per year)\n- Consider PRP injection if refractory\n\n**6. Long-Term Upper Extremity Preservation**:\n- Annual shoulder screening exam\n- Median nerve protection (carpal tunnel is common in chronic SCI — avoid prolonged wrist hyperextension)\n- Ergonomic desk/computer setup for work\n- Electric door openers, reaching aids, and other environmental modifications to reduce overhead reaching',
