      content:
          '**Letter of Medical Necessity (LMN) — Key Components**:\n\n1. **Diagnosis and medical history**: C6 ASIA A SCI x 10 years, bilateral shoulder tendinopathy, healed Stage 2 sacral pressure injury\n2. **Functional limitations**: The patient\'s current wheelchair is 8 years old with frame fatigue. A standard wheelchair does NOT meet his needs because:\n- Standard wheelchair weight (35-50 lbs) would accelerate shoulder deterioration\n- Standard wheelchair lacks proper axle adjustability for propulsion efficiency\n- Standard sling seat/back causes posterior pelvic tilt → pressure injury recurrence risk\n3. **Medical necessity for ultralight**: Ultralight classification (≤30 lbs) is medically necessary to reduce propulsion forces on damaged rotator cuffs. Literature supports that ultralight wheelchairs reduce stroke frequency by 10-15% compared to standard wheelchairs.\n4. **Medical necessity for power assist**: Progressive bilateral rotator cuff tendinopathy despite conservative management. Power-assist reduces propulsion effort by 30-50%, documented to slow progression of upper extremity deterioration in SCI.\n5. **Seating system justification**: Prior Stage 2 sacral pressure injury with pressure mapping demonstrating peak pressures >180 mmHg on current cushion. ROHO Quadtro Select provides superior pressure redistribution, documented on repeat pressure mapping.\n\n**Assistive Technology Professional (ATP) Certification**:\n- **CMS requires** that complex rehabilitation technology (Group 3 power wheelchairs, custom seating) be prescribed with involvement of a **RESNA-certified ATP**\n- The ATP conducts the seating evaluation, recommends specific products, ensures proper fit, and provides documentation supporting medical necessity\n- The ATP and physician must independently document the evaluation\n\n**Medicare/CMS Requirements for Complex Rehabilitation Technology**:\n- **Face-to-face evaluation**: Within 6 months of prescription by the treating physician\n- **Detailed Product Description (DPD)**: Specific product codes (HCPCS), features, and justification for each component\n- **Functional mobility assessment**: Document why a lower-level device is insufficient\n- **SADMERC (Statistical Analysis Durable Medical Equipment Regional Carrier)**: Previously handled coding verification; now replaced by **Pricing, Data Analysis and Coding (PDAC)** contractor. Each wheelchair and component must have a valid HCPCS code verified by PDAC.\n- **Competitive Bidding**: Complex rehabilitation technology is currently exempt from Medicare competitive bidding (advocated by NRRTS and industry groups)\n\n**Appeal Process for Denial**:\n1. **First-level appeal**: Submit detailed LMN with pressure mapping data, shoulder imaging reports, and clinical documentation. Cite peer-reviewed literature supporting ultralight wheelchair and power-assist for shoulder preservation.\n2. **Peer-to-peer review**: Request to speak directly with the insurance medical director. Physiatrist-to-physician communication is often effective.\n3. **External review/state insurance commissioner**: If internal appeals fail.\n4. **Patient advocacy**: Connect with United Spinal Association or state disability advocacy organizations for assistance navigating appeals.',
